From 8d500e264497b8640a5ef5b5b4b369ff3a0a72e7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mita5358 <kafitcohr@outlook.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 16:15:23 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 02/20] perf(dsh): restore folded rows in one pass instead of a
 scan per row
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

"Load earlier" on the DSH backend is a synchronous click handler budgeted at
<=50 ms (docs/codex-backend-design.md §10.7), but foldBack() ran one full
linear scan of the restore window per folded row. A titan session folds tens
of thousands of rows past MAX_ROWS, so one press took seconds: 2100 ms at
~18k folded rows on a real session log, 1366 ms on the 20k-row fixture.

Build the `seq -> tool/call` index and the assistant/message cursor in a
single bounded pass instead, and answer every row from a map hit or a binary
search: O(events + folded × log messages) instead of O(folded × events).

This restores exactly what the per-row scan did, including the behaviour that
scan got by accident:

  - a non-call event sharing a tool row's seq must not shadow a real call —
    the scan filtered to `tool/call` and kept looking, so the index does too;
  - a text row takes the FIRST assistant/message at or after its anchor;
  - seqs below the first folded row never answer.

The previous implementation is kept verbatim as an oracle in
scripts/verify-dsh-fold-back.ts and diffed against the new one (row state and
restored count), alongside a 20k randomized differential run.

Same 20k-row fixture, median of five presses: 1366 ms -> 32 ms.
Real titan session (18,042 folded rows): 2100 ms -> 42 ms.

Run: node --import tsx/esm scripts/verify-dsh-fold-back.ts  (19 checks)
